{"uid":"8e126c347cd877da","name":"test_run_bounded_reaches_a_grandchild_through_a_term_obeying_parent","fullName":"tests.test_run_bounded_timeout_marker#test_run_bounded_reaches_a_grandchild_through_a_term_obeying_parent","historyId":"07f7214568eb75d3c5f69f47e66760a2","time":{"start":1791941847795,"stop":1791941849811,"duration":2016},"description":"Task 010 (M.3, operator steer 008): the complementary case to task\n    009's `test_run_bounded_reaches_a_grandchild_with_pgrep_unreachable_through_path`,\n    which wraps a command that ITSELF ignores TERM. Here the wrapped command\n    has no TERM trap at all -- it obeys the very first TERM it receives and\n    dies immediately -- but it has already spawned a background grandchild\n    (its own `sleep 30 &`) before that TERM arrives. `kill_tree` walks\n    depth-first and signals every descendant it finds strictly BEFORE the\n    pid it was called on, so the grandchild is (in this walk order) signalled\n    ahead of the TERM-obeying parent -- but only if the walk can still find\n    it. With `pgrep` unreachable and `children_of()` reverted to a\n    pgrep-only walk, `children_of(cmd_pid)` finds nothing, so `kill_tree`\n    sends TERM to the parent alone; the parent (obeying it) exits at once,\n    but that leaves the grandchild orphaned and still running its own 30s\n    sleep, holding this step's stdout pipe open long after the parent -- and\n    this script's own report -- have already finished. A TERM-obeying parent\n    proves only that this tree's ROOT was reaped, never that the rest of the\n    tree was too; this pins the latter with a parent that dies on its own\n    the instant it is signalled, so the only thing that can still be left\n    running is a grandchild the walk failed to reach.","descriptionHtml":"<p>Task 010 (M.3, operator steer 008): the complementary case to task<br />\n009's <code>test_run_bounded_reaches_a_grandchild_with_pgrep_unreachable_through_path</code>,<br />\nwhich wraps a command that ITSELF ignores TERM. Here the wrapped command<br />\nhas no TERM trap at all -- it obeys the very first TERM it receives and<br />\ndies immediately -- but it has already spawned a background grandchild<br />\n(its own <code>sleep 30 &amp;</code>) before that TERM arrives. <code>kill_tree</code> walks<br />\ndepth-first and signals every descendant it finds strictly BEFORE the<br />\npid it was called on, so the grandchild is (in this walk order) signalled<br />\nahead of the TERM-obeying parent -- but only if the walk can still find<br />\nit. With <code>pgrep</code> unreachable and <code>children_of()</code> reverted to a<br />\npgrep-only walk, <code>children_of(cmd_pid)</code> finds nothing, so <code>kill_tree</code><br />\nsends TERM to the parent alone; the parent (obeying it) exits at once,<br />\nbut that leaves the grandchild orphaned and still running its own 30s<br />\nsleep, holding this step's stdout pipe open long after the parent -- and<br />\nthis script's own report -- have already finished. A TERM-obeying parent<br />\nproves only that this tree's ROOT was reaped, never that the rest of the<br />\ntree was too; this pins the latter with a parent that dies on its own<br />\nthe instant it is signalled, so the only thing that can still be left<br />\nrunning is a grandchild the walk failed to reach.</p>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791941650367,"stop":1791941650367,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791941650367,"stop":1791941650367,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachme